From 8f74a3a6829d6573b0fec196d3216a1899f34194 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: LukeOkk <85941941+LukeOkk@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 30 Sep 2026 02:06:17 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 1/9] build, libartbase: add the heap reference base (SteamARM,
 HYPOTHESIS)

HYPOTHESIS, UNTESTED: not compiled, not run. Part of SteamARM's study of an
ART whose managed heap lives in [base, base + 4 GiB) instead of the low
4 GiB (docs/ART_HEAP_ABOVE_4GB.md in SteamARM), for arm64 macOS processes,
where nothing can be mapped below 4 GiB.

ART_HEAP_REFERENCE_BASE (environment, a hex number such as 0x4000000000)
defines two constants:

- kHeapReferenceBase: what this runtime uses (device builds, C++ and
  assembly; 0 on the host and in 32-bit builds);
- kTargetHeapReferenceBase: what the arm64 code generator emits (host and
  device builds of the compiler).

A compressed reference stays the low 32 bits of the address; decompression
ORs the base back in and keeps 0 as null. The base is 0 or a single bit
between 2^32 and 2^46, so the 32-bit store of a pointer is the compression
and one ORR (a logical immediate) the decompression.

PointerToLowMemUInt32() now means "the compressed address in the window";
LowMemUInt32ToPointer() is its inverse. With the variable unset nothing
changes.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01L6ANQQienC2qRCeZBzUfuS
